dataptrs_arch_*.so -- shared objects whose data pointers are listed by each
machine's own dynamic relocations (GH #1502).

What they are
  Built from dataptrs_arch.c (a hidden function, an exported function and an
  exported int, a pointer to each function, a GOT read of the int, a PLT call
  of the export). Not executed. SHA-256 and the relocations `llvm-readelf -r`
  prints (slot type target):

  dataptrs_arch_riscv64.so
    ffa0e909f5efce29bb48bf13e8fb8ecde8cab22ea7caf747264b8606e472524d
    0x3568 R_RISCV_RELATIVE 0x13d0 (audit_slot -> audit_target)
    0x3570 R_RISCV_64 audit_export (audit_export_slot)
    0x2558 R_RISCV_64 audit_data (GOT)
    0x3588 R_RISCV_JUMP_SLOT audit_export
  dataptrs_arch_riscv32.so
    01908df6c774bf28e87c251cd3bf50db22c98a98ac1c16c73ca040637231ae3e
    0x339c R_RISCV_RELATIVE 0x1280, 0x33a0 R_RISCV_32 audit_export,
    0x2394 R_RISCV_32 audit_data, 0x33ac R_RISCV_JUMP_SLOT audit_export
  dataptrs_arch_ppc32.so (secure PLT: DT_PPC_GOT 0x204c8)
    6d67491ca2f501fc444d524aae0f1424744fbb288971705457432685af1ecdce
    0x304dc R_PPC_RELATIVE 0x10338, 0x304e0 R_PPC_ADDR32 audit_export,
    0x304d4 R_PPC_ADDR32 audit_data, 0x304e4 R_PPC_JMP_SLOT audit_export
  dataptrs_arch_ppc32_bssplt.so (BSS PLT: no DT_PPC_GOT, .plt is code)
    ba2e5a284ed1f22569b7fac5c068ce9acc83e650c30defb79c87f12b92366992
    0x2014 R_PPC_RELATIVE 0x230, 0x2018 R_PPC_ADDR32 audit_export,
    0x1f6c R_PPC_ADDR32 audit_data, 0x2064 R_PPC_JMP_SLOT audit_export
  dataptrs_arch_ppc64le.so (ELFv2, e_flags 2)
    8e33125de184bd372340b26c441feaa277aa6ada8677b7eac30821cd5b2c12fc
    0x306a0 R_PPC64_RELATIVE 0x10480, 0x306a8 R_PPC64_ADDR64 audit_export,
    0x20690 R_PPC64_ADDR64 audit_data, 0x306c0 R_PPC64_JMP_SLOT audit_export
  dataptrs_arch_ppc64_elfv1.so (big-endian ELFv1, e_flags 1)
    211ca020df8b8f89cdb4bafd33bf725fe9d533044d043c8fe69fde21f7deca09
    .opd descriptors 0x1ea0/0x1eb8/0x1ed0/0x1ee8, each an R_PPC64_RELATIVE
    entry word and TOC word; 0x2038 R_PPC64_RELATIVE 0x1ea0 (audit_slot ->
    audit_target's descriptor), 0x2040 R_PPC64_ADDR64 audit_export (its
    descriptor 0x1eb8), 0x1f08 R_PPC64_ADDR64 audit_data, 0x2018
    R_PPC64_JMP_SLOT audit_export (a 24-byte descriptor copy, not a word)

How they were built
  LLVM: Ubuntu clang 14.0.0 compiling, LLD 23.1.1 (rust-lld) linking:
    clang --target=<triple> -fuse-ld=lld --ld-path=<ld.lld> -fPIC -shared \
      -nostdlib -O0 -Wl,--build-id=none dataptrs_arch.c -o <out>
  with <triple> riscv64-unknown-linux-gnu, riscv32-unknown-linux-gnu,
  powerpc-unknown-linux-gnu, powerpc64le-unknown-linux-gnu.
  GNU: powerpc-linux-gnu-gcc 13.3.0, GNU ld 2.42:
    powerpc-linux-gnu-gcc [-mbss-plt | -m64] -O0 -fPIC -shared -nostdlib \
      -Wl,--build-id=none -Wl,-z,max-page-size=0x1000 \
      -Wl,-z,common-page-size=0x1000 dataptrs_arch.c -o <out>
  Prebuilt so the relocation kinds do not depend on the host toolchain.

What they pin
  kuna_dataptrs lists every relocated word whose relocation stores a word with
  coverage dynamic-relocations: RISC-V's absolute GOT words and JUMP_SLOT,
  PowerPC's RELATIVE/ADDR words and its PLT slot only where it is a word
  (secure PLT, ELFv2). `kuna xrefs` reports them as pointer rows. The SHT_RELR
  slots of relr_so_x86_64.so are listed the same way. Corrupted copies (a
  .rela.dyn sh_link, a PE base-relocation directory RVA) are made at test time
  from dataptrs_so_x86_64.so and dataptrs_textptr_i386.exe.
